# ⚠⚠ THIS FILE EXISTS BECAUSE A DEPLOY SHIPPED TESTNET TO PRODUCTION.
#
# `vercel --prod` uploads this directory and Vercel builds from it. `.gitignore`
# DOES NOT STOP THAT — there was no `.vercelignore`, so `.env.local` went up with
# everything else, Next loaded it, and it beat the project's own environment.
# `.env.local` was pointed at BNB testnet at the time, so bnbulls.xyz went live
# on chain 97: every wallet got "wrong network, switch to bnb testnet" and the
# revive ladder read $0.10 instead of $50. 2026-08-11, ~4 minutes, rolled back.
#
# ⚠ `.env.local` ALSO CARRIES SECRETS — `ADMIN_SESSION_SECRET` and a testnet
# `BNBULLS_SIGNER_KEY_97`. Uploading it did not just break the chain config, it
# overrode the real production admin session secret with a local dev one. The
# comment inside `.env.local` claims "this file is not deployed". It was.
#
# So: local env files never leave this machine. Everything production needs
# lives in the Vercel project environment (`vercel env ls production`), which is
# the only source that cannot change depending on whose laptop ran the deploy.
.env
.env.*
!.env.example

# Build/test scratch that has no business in a deployment.
.next/cache
*.log

# ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# ⚠⚠ READ THIS BEFORE TOUCHING PRODUCTION ENV: `vercel.json` WINS. ALWAYS.
# ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# `vercel.json`'s `build.env` is the HIGHEST-priority env source on Vercel. It
# beats the project's own environment variables AND it beats `.env.local`.
# `vercel.json` cannot hold comments (Vercel rejects unknown keys, `_comment`
# included), which is why this warning lives here.
#
# On 2026-08-11 that file was still pinned to BNB TESTNET (chain 97) while the
# site served mainnet. FOUR production deploys shipped testnet to bnbulls.xyz
# because of it: "wrong network, switch to bnb testnet" in every wallet, revive
# ladder reading $0.10 instead of $50. Each was caught and rolled back in about
# ten seconds, but the point is what did NOT help — fixing `.env.local`, adding
# all 21 NEXT_PUBLIC vars to the Vercel project, deleting `.next`, and passing
# `--force`. Every one of those loses to `vercel.json`.
#
# So the chain config lives in `vercel.json`, on mainnet, in git where it can be
# reviewed. `.env.local` is for `next dev` only and never reaches a build.
# If a contract is redeployed, `vercel.json` changes in the SAME commit.
#
# Verify EVERY production deploy before trusting it:
#   curl -s https://bnbulls.xyz/ | grep -o '0x[aA]8[dD]00[fF]9b[a-fA-F0-9]*'
# That is the mainnet $BNBULL address. If the testnet one (0x12b10E53...)
# comes back, roll back immediately:
#   vercel rollback <last-good-deployment-url> --yes
